# What gets published.
#
# vercel.json sets outputDirectory ".", so a deploy serves the REPO ROOT - and
# this repo also holds the mobile app's source, which arrived when its history
# was merged in as the reference implementation to port from.
#
# This file used to be a denylist. That published every top-level directory
# nobody had thought to name: backend/src/prompts.ts, gamification/, hooks/,
# eas.json and google-services.json (with a live Firebase key in it) were all
# reachable on beta over plain HTTP. Production escaped only because it was
# last deployed before the merge.
#
# So: deny everything, then name what the web app actually needs. A new
# top-level directory is now invisible to the deploy until someone adds it
# here on purpose. deploy-manifest.spec.js pins the list below.
/*

!/index.html
!/favicon.ico
!/CNAME
!/vercel.json

!/js
!/css
!/assets
!/api

# Inside an included directory, so it needs saying explicitly. The functions
# ship to Vercel; their tests do not.
/api/**/__tests__
